---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About this self-hosting catalog"
  description="What kokoapi.space is, how entries are chosen, what the resource figures mean, and how to contribute a new app."
>
  <div class="container page-head prose-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About the catalog</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      kokoapi.space is a static, Markdown-driven directory of software you can run on your
      own hardware. No database, no accounts, no tracking and no AI-generated reviews — just
      facts, links and comparisons.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="container prose-page">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Why another catalog?</h2>
      <p>
        Most directories are either walls of links or vote-driven lists where resource usage,
        licensing and packaging details are buried on the project's own page. This catalog
        treats those details as first-class data — every app is tagged with its license,
        architecture support, typical RAM/CPU footprint, database requirements, Docker and
        Kubernetes packaging, and SSO capabilities — so you can filter to the apps that
        actually fit your hardware.
      </p>

      <h2>How entries are chosen</h2>
      <p>An app qualifies when it is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Actively maintained and realistically self-hostable by one person or a small team;</li>
        <li>Deployable with Docker and/or Kubernetes (or trivially installable on Linux);</li>
        <li>Licensed under terms that permit self-hosting without surprise;</li>
        <li>Not primarily a thin wrapper around a proprietary third-party service.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to read the specs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>RAM and CPU figures</strong> are typical <em>baseline</em> values for a
          small, single-node deployment — not certified minimums or peak usage. Always check
          the project's own requirements before committing hardware.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Docker / Kubernetes</strong> means a maintained official or widely adopted
          image, chart or manifest set exists. It is not a promise that every feature works
          in every orchestrator.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SSO</strong> means the app supports standard external login
          (OIDC / SAML / LDAP) out of the box; community features like self-service
          registration are separate questions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>License</strong> refers to the self-hosted edition. Some projects also
          offer paid modules under different terms — check before deploying for business use.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The site is built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> as 100% static HTML.
        Content lives in this Git repository as plain Markdown files (one per app and per
        category). Building produces a small set of HTML/CSS/JS files that are served by
        nginx in a hardened container — no server-side runtime at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Contributing</h2>
      <p>
        Add a Markdown file to <code>content/products</code> (or improve an existing entry)
        and open a pull request. The schema is small and documented in
        <code>src/content.config.ts</code>. Corrections are very welcome — figures change as
        projects evolve.
      </p>

      <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
      <p>
        This is a community-maintained reference, not a security audit or a recommendation
        to run any specific software. Verify licenses, review code you deploy, keep systems
        updated, and expose services only through a properly configured reverse proxy.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
